beyond will too so my pick for today  we're looking at concerns about  returning to the office during this  pandemic according to linkedin  more than two-thirds of working  professionals say their workplaces  are now open nearly one-third say they  are required to return  but a majority 57 percent say they are  not comfortable doing so well we spoke  to a fitness instructor a teacher and a  former corporate pr manager  who shared their concerns about going  back to their workplaces  i'm a fitness professional when gyms  opened back up i had some concerns about  my health and safety we were expected to  return to work during covet for 75  of our wages and it felt like a real  devaluing of life  my feeling in going back to the  classroom is nervousness  i have three children my position was  eliminated in my corporate job  that represented a significant portion  of our family income  and our health care the scary thought  occurred to me  of what happens if we go back in person  and one of my students get sick  and maybe even dies am i  prepared to handle something like that  no  with knowing that school is going to be  virtual and i have a special needs  learner  it makes it very difficult for me to go  look for another full-time position  not knowing what the company's policies  will be covet has  made just living really anxiety-inducing  in our new normal series we're looking  at how  the coronavirus is changing our lives  every day  and how we can reduce anxiety about  going back to the workplace well we're  joined by linkedin editor at large  caroline  fairchild she joins us from her home in  brooklyn good morning to you  you know one of the first questions i  have is what assurances do  employees need to feel safe as they get  ready to return back to work  what assurances can the companies give  the employees  sure we're hearing through this survey  that we did of workers across the  country that employees are really  looking for their employers to create  coveted 19 specific policies to make  sure that a return to work is safe  a vast majority of employers are  requiring that their workers wear a mask  all day in the office  many are banning in-person meetings and  there's also staggered start so you  might not get to the office at the same  time as your colleagues  all of these are a way to make sure that  as offices are reopening workers are  feeling safe we're seeing a lot of  stress and anxiety right now as workers  are asked to return to work you spoke  with lots of workers across industries  and  employers are having to really think  about how to make sure that workers feel  safe and can  continue to be productive in the office  aside from just wearing the mask which  even our  our crew here they're all wearing masks  we make sure that we're maintaining  social distancing  what are some of the specific concerns  you're hearing though from parents  parents are telling us since the  beginning of this pandemic that working  productively from home has been a huge  challenge  over a third of the workers that we  surveyed in a recent survey told us that  they haven't felt productive at home  while balancing child care but  the rub is there is that a return to  office may not solve some of these  problems  over 30 percent of working parents told  us that if they had to return to the  office tomorrow they wouldn't have child  care options available  and get this 60 of workers are saying  that their employers are not allowing  them to make accommodations in their  schedule to  make up for the fact that schools may  not reopen and that they don't have  child care options available so this is  a big concern  i put a post on linkedin last week and  within an hour heard from  hundreds of working parents like some of  those that you spoke with and they're  just saying that there's so much stress  and anxiety they don't know what to do  and so they're having to have critical  conversations with their manager  about really what is next and how they  can continue to be productive during  this new normal  what does the data reveal about women in  particular and balancing  work and child care at the same time  millennial women have been  disproportionately affected by this  crisis we know this by looking at  linkedin data and looking at hiring data  as well as  who's applying for jobs right now so if  you think of millennial women these are  the women  that are most likely to have young kids  at home right now and they're getting  hired at a slower rate than their male  peers right now which is concerning  particularly in male dominant industries  like tech finance  media these are industries that were  struggling with hiring women before and  now they're hiring even fewer during  this pandemic  and we're also seeing millennial women  apply to jobs at a slower rate and that  probably has a lot to do with the fact  that  as one working mother wrote on linkedin  she's the default parent right now  through this pandemic so if she's  working and her husband's working she's  taking on  a vast majority of the child care needs  and if employers don't figure out how to  make accommodations for working parents  right now  it's going to have a long-term impact on  all of their careers and you have people  also on the other side that are not as  fearful  and eager to go back to work what are  you hearing from from those  people sure so we've looked at data  across  industries and construction workers are  actually funny enough  they're excited about the prospect of  returning to work and that probably has  a lot to do with the fact there are lots  of safety procedures  on job sites already to make sure that  social distance is maintained and  workers are safe  retail workers as you can imagine  they're the most hesitant right now to  return to work because of all the touch  points with customers  and we're seeing a lot of office jobs  they're switching a lot of what they're  doing to remote work  telehealth is we're switching a lot to  remote right now as well as a lot of  administrative roles  and learning education roles we're  seeing a lot of postings on linkedin for  e-learning  options so there's some adaptations that  are happening so that workers are  spending less time in the office and  then we're seeing across  industries a gamut of different feelings  about returning to work  and obviously people need money they  need to go back to work eventually  what do you tell the folks who are  really concerned about saying  something to their employee about their  concerns and maybe even fearful that  they could lose their job if they  complain or have  real concerns about returning  what i'd say to that employee is that  leaders really have to lead with empathy  right now the number one trait that we  see experts saying on linkedin that  managers and employers need to think  about right now is empathetic leadership  and that means  putting yourself in the shoes of your  employee if you think about it if you  have a worker who is anxious and nervous  about the prospect of returning  to work there's no way that they're  going to be productive so we're seeing a  lot of employers make accommodations  bring more flexibility to the job to  make sure that employers feel safe and  feel like they can be productive  and we're seeing just go to your manager  with a clear proposal if they're anxious  about the fact that your team is going  to be the office and you're going to  continue to work from home  address that head on and say i'll do  more video calls i'll send an email at  the end of the day letting you know  what i've been doing i'll give you  access to my calendar there are ways  that in this new normal we can continue  to work from hope and do so productively  well we've made it work this long so  good luck to everyone out there trying  to make it work  carolyn fairchild thank you so much for  helping us understand it all
 

